.TH JOIN 1.SH NAMEjoin \- join two sorted files on a common field.SH SYNOPSIS\fBjoin\fR[\-a 1|2] [\-v 1|2] [\-e string] [\-o list] [\-t char][\-1 field] [\-2 field] file1 file2.SH DESCRIPTION\fBjoin\fRis GNU join, from the textutils.  It reads two files, each sorted on itsjoin field, and writes one line for every pair of lines whose joinfields are equal: the join field, then the rest of the line from\fIfile1\fR,\fRthen the rest from\fIfile2\fR.\fRFields are separated by blanks, and the join field is the first.Either file may be\fB\-\fRfor the standard input..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-a\fI n\fRalso print the lines of file\fIn\fR(1 or 2) that have no partner..TP 12\fB\-v\fI n\fRprint ONLY the lines of file\fIn\fRthat have no partner..TP 12\fB\-e\fI string\fRput\fIstring\fRin any output field that is missing..TP 12\fB\-o\fI list\fRprint the fields in\fIlist\fRinstead: each entry is\fIfile\fR.\fIfield\fR,\fRas in\fB1.2,2.2\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-t\fI char\fRfields are separated by\fIchar\fRrather than blanks..TP 12\fB\-1 \fIf\fP, \-2 \fIf\fP\fRjoin on field\fIf\fRof file 1, or of file 2.\fB\-j1\fRand\fB\-j2\fRare the older spellings..SH EXAMPLESGiven\fBfruit\fRholding `1 apple', `2 pear', `3 plum' and\fBcolour\fRholding `1 red', `3 purple', `4 blue':.nfjoin fruit colour            1 apple red                             3 plum purplejoin -v 2 fruit colour       4 bluejoin -o 1.2,2.2 fruit colour apple red                             plum purplejoin -t , a.csv b.csv        comma-separated fields.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fRSort both files on the join field first; sort's own options will do it..SH SEE ALSOsort, uniq